SX-198.plan:
GOAL: fix #198 and the same weakness everywhere. Today 54 files guard against being opened directly with basename($_SERVER['SCRIPT_FILENAME']) === basename(__FILE__). A library with the same file name as an entry point therefore redirects and exits when that entry point loads it. web/_functions/cron.php versus web/Go2My.Link/_admin/public_html/cron.php is live today. API handlers already share names with libraries (analytics.php, org.php, account.php, urls.php), which is one rename away from the same failure.

STEP 1 — list them: grep -rl "=== basename(__FILE__)" web | grep -v _libraries (54 files on 2026-09-21, including web/_includes/*, web/_functions/*, web/G2My.Link/_functions/*, web/Go2My.Link/_functions/*, web/Lnks.page/_functions/*, the six API handlers, install/index.php and web/_auth_keys/auth_creds.example.php). Also check for variants: grep -rn "basename(\$_SERVER\['SCRIPT_FILENAME'\]" web | grep -v _libraries.

STEP 2 — replace each guard with exactly this block. Keep the file's existing '🛡️ Direct Access Guard' heading comment and add the reason once per file:
// Compares the real resolved paths, not just the file names. Comparing names
// wrongly fired when a different file with the same name (for example the
// admin cron.php endpoint loading this cron.php library) was the script being
// run, which redirected before any code ran (#198). realpath('') returns the
// current folder, hence the empty check.
$g2mlGuardScriptPath = (string) ($_SERVER['SCRIPT_FILENAME'] ?? '');
if ($g2mlGuardScriptPath !== '' && realpath($g2mlGuardScriptPath) === realpath(__FILE__))
{
    header('Location: https://go2my.link');
    exit;
}
unset($g2mlGuardScriptPath);
Keep each file's existing redirect target if it differs from https://go2my.link. For install/index.php (an entry point whose guard compares with itself), keep the same new form. Behaviour when the file really is opened directly is unchanged.

STEP 3 — regression test tests/unit/direct_access_guard_test.php (DB-free):
(a) Static check: no file under web/ outside any _libraries folder contains "basename($_SERVER['SCRIPT_FILENAME']" (print offenders in the failure message).
(b) Subprocess test (the only meaningful proof: PHP defines functions before the guard runs, so checking function_exists proves nothing). Write a temporary PHP script into sys_get_temp_dir() that sets $_SERVER['SCRIPT_FILENAME'] = <absolute path of web/Go2My.Link/_admin/public_html/cron.php>, defines the constants cron.php needs at include time (check its top; it should need none), does require '<repo>/web/_functions/cron.php'; then echo 'AFTER_REQUIRE_OK';. Run it with exec(escapeshellarg(PHP_BINARY) . ' ' . escapeshellarg($tmp) . ' 2>&1', $out, $code). Assert the output contains AFTER_REQUIRE_OK. Delete the temp file in a finally block.
(c) The same subprocess check with SCRIPT_FILENAME set to the library's own path: the output must NOT contain AFTER_REQUIRE_OK (the guard still protects direct opening).
(d) Repeat (b) for web/_functions/analytics.php with SCRIPT_FILENAME = web/Go2My.Link/public_html/api/v1/handlers/analytics.php, to lock the name-sharing case.
If exec() is disabled in the environment, skip (b)–(d) with a clear 'SKIPPED: exec disabled' message rather than passing silently.

STEP 4 — tests/README.md: one line describing the new test. docs/DEPLOYMENT.md cron section: the endpoint now actually runs (it still ships switched off; #178 wires the trigger). Post the issue comment.

VERIFY: php -l on every changed file (loop over the list; read each exit code); php tests/run.php and read its exit code directly; grep shows 0 old-style guards.
